<!-- Scheduling: Audience Calendars Section -->
<div class="card">
	<h3 id="scheduling-audiences" class="ffc-icon-users"><?php esc_html_e( 'Audience Calendars', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h3>

	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Audience calendars book shared spaces (rooms, equipment, "ambientes") for named groups of people, rather than fixed one-on-one slots. Everything lives under the Scheduling menu.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Audiences (groups)', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'An audience is a named group of members, with a color, an active/inactive status and an optional 3-level parent hierarchy. Members are imported or self-join (when allowed): an audience can let users join or leave its child groups from their dashboard, with a cap on how many self-join groups one user may belong to. Audiences also carry the custom fields used by reregistration.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <a href="#feature-audiences"><?php esc_html_e( 'See Audience Custom Fields', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></a>.</p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The tree has at most three levels: a top-level audience, its groups and their sub-groups. An audience cannot be placed under itself or one of its own groups, and moving an audience moves its groups with it, so a move that would push one of them to a fourth level is refused with the reason. The same rule holds for the CSV import, where such a row is skipped with the reason.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A top-level audience with child groups sets self-join in one choice of four: Off, This audience only, This audience + children and grandchildren, or Children and grandchildren only. The choice is written to the audience and to every group below it; with the audience itself closed, the dashboard shows it as a heading over the groups users can join. When the groups below disagree, no option is selected and a notice gives the audience\'s own state and how many of its children and grandchildren allow self-join; saving leaves everything as it is until you pick an option. A group created under the audience starts with self-join on when every existing child and grandchild allows it, and off otherwise, including under an audience with no groups yet. An audience without child groups keeps one switch.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'On the new-audience form, picking a parent audience replaces the self-join switch with "Inherits from the audience above.": the new group takes the value described above.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The cap is set under Scheduling → Settings → Audience Calendars → Self-Join, "Groups per user": from 1 to 20, 2 by default. Every membership in a group that allows self-join counts, however it was added; a user at the cap leaves a group before joining another, and the dashboard says how many groups they may join.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Two more switches on a top-level audience cover it and every child and grandchild; the groups below show the state read-only. "Visible to members only" hides the whole audience from the dashboard\'s Join groups panel for users who belong to none of its groups, however they were added (by you, by an import or by self-join), and the server refuses a join request from them; calendars, bookings and the rest of the dashboard are not affected. "Keep at least one group", available only while the first is on, stops a member from leaving the last group they hold in the audience: its Leave button is disabled with a line saying why and the server refuses the request. "Leave all groups" still leaves every other group, but in such an audience it keeps the group the user joined first (the earliest membership) and leaves the rest of it; a notice then names each group kept and the audience that requires it. Removing members on this screen is not affected.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'When a user is at the cap, or holds only one group of an audience that keeps its members, the other self-join groups of that audience offer "Switch to this": in one step the user leaves a group they hold there and joins the new one. With several groups held there, the dashboard asks which one to replace. A switch only moves between groups of the same top-level audience, so it never takes a user past the cap.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Joining a group brings its upcoming bookings into the user\'s list at once, and the dashboard says how many. Before leaving one, the confirmation says how many upcoming bookings leave the list with it: those that reach the user through that group alone, not those that also name them or another of their groups. A cancelled booking shows, under its description, the reason the person who cancelled it gave.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Schedules & environments', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A schedule is a bookable calendar; inside it, each environment is a space (room/resource) with its own weekly working hours (default 08:00–18:00, per-day closable) and color. A schedule controls visibility, how far ahead bookings are allowed, per-user booking permissions, and the notification settings below.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'A new calendar starts public.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p class="description"><?php esc_html_e( 'On a record that already exists, a switch saves the moment you flip it, without the Save button. A new record keeps the Save button until it is created.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'The editors', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A calendar is edited in vertical tabs: General, Notifications and Event list & badges, then, once the calendar is saved, its environments (the Environments list of that calendar), User access and Closed dates. One Save button beside the tabs saves the first three together; the shortcode sits beside it.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'Add New on the Environments tab opens the environment form with that calendar already chosen.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'An audience is edited in three tabs: Audience, Members (add and remove members) and Reregistration fields. The member count in the audiences list opens the Members tab.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The Members tab and a calendar\'s User access tab show each person\'s account email masked, for everyone. Holders of ffc_view_audiences_pii (the Audience Calendars Manager and Administrator roles, FFC Administrator and WordPress administrators) get a Reveal button beside each address and "Reveal this page" above the members list, and every reveal writes an Activity Log entry. The Viewer and Operator roles see the email masked.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The custom users listed in a booking\'s details on Audience Bookings follow the same rule, with the same Reveal. The user search that adds members, custom users and calendar access shows each address masked, and only holders of ffc_view_audiences_pii can search by email address; everyone else searches by name or username.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<?php ob_start(); ?>
		<p>
			<?php esc_html_e( 'Unlike personal calendars, an audience booking has a free start/end time. Two bookings that overlap the same environment are a hard conflict (blocked); overlaps for the same audience or user are soft conflicts the booker can acknowledge. One check reports both kinds together, and names up to ten of the people who are booked elsewhere at that time, with how many more there are. Each booking needs a short description (15–300 characters).', 'ffcertificate' ); ?>
		</p>
	<?php
	\FreeFormCertificate\Core\AdminNotice::callout(
		'info',
		(string) ob_get_clean(),
		array( 'title' => __( 'Free-form times, not fixed slots.', 'ffcertificate' ) )
	);
	?>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Booking flow', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Publish a calendar with the shortcode:', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <code>[ffc_audience schedule_id="0" environment_id="0" view="month"]</code>. <?php esc_html_e( 'schedule_id/environment_id of 0 show everything the visitor may access; view is month or week. Bookings are made through the plugin\'s REST API and can target one or more audiences or an individual, and are cancelled with a required reason.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'The Shortcode column of Audience Calendars shows each calendar\'s shortcode with a copy button, and the Environments view shows each environment\'s, tied to its calendar.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The booking dialog asks when, where and for whom. One day, Several days or Weekly sets the span; several days adds the last day, how the hours read on each day and the switch that skips blocked days, and weekly adds the last day the series may reach. Audiences are ticked in a searchable list that shows each one\'s colour and how many people a booking for it reaches, a person in several sub-audiences counted once; ticking an audience ticks its sub-audiences too. One button checks the conflicts and, once the check passes, creates the booking; changing the date, the times, the space or who it is for asks for a new check. On a phone the dialog rises from the bottom of the screen and the audience list opens as a screen of its own.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A booking reaches its audiences\' members: the e-mail, the dashboard, the check-in and the post-event form. So the server checks who it names. A member books only for the audiences they belong to, or for one of those audiences\' parents, which is what the dialog offers. Booking for individual users takes ffc_manage_audiences, and only those who hold it see the choice. A WordPress administrator or a holder of ffc_bypass_audiences may book any audience. Every audience and user named must exist.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Booking rules', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Every booking is checked on the server, whoever makes it: the calendar and the space must be active; the date must not be in the past, beyond the calendar\'s future days limit (counted in the site\'s time zone), a global or calendar holiday, or a day the space is closed; and both the start and the end must fall inside the space\'s opening hours. An all-day booking takes the space\'s whole opening window for that day. Two bookings may not overlap in one space.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A booking can cover a range of days: set an end date in the booking form and the booking takes every day from its date to that one. The hours on each day are either the same every day, or run from the start time on the first day to the end time on the last, with the whole opening window in between. Every day is checked against the rules and for conflicts; a clash on any day refuses the whole booking, and the form lists the days already taken. A holiday or a weekday the space is closed inside the range refuses the booking unless "Skip blocked days" is ticked, and then those days are left out without a warning and recorded as skipped. A range covers at most 30 consecutive days unless the limit is changed under Scheduling → Settings → Audience Calendars → Bookings over several days, "Longest range" (1 to 365). A longer range is refused with a message naming the limit, already by the conflict check; an all-day booking covers one day.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'When the hours run from the first day to the last, the calendar, the booking details, the user dashboard and the emails show the hours each day actually takes, and the calendar file covers every booked day. Cancelling a range cancels every day it covers; the confirmation names them and enables its button after a short countdown.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Weekly repeats one day: the same day of the week, space, hours and people every week until the "Repeat until" date, for up to 52 weeks. The check runs the rules and the room\'s conflicts on each date and lists the dates that cannot be booked, with why; those dates are left out, and the rest are booked. A date in a weekly series is never booked over a conflict or a closed day, even by someone who may override: that stays a decision about one booking. Each date is an ordinary booking, with its own attendance, post-event form and cancellation, and the participants receive one e-mail that lists every date, with a calendar file holding them all.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'With every space of a calendar shown ("All" in the space filter), a day is open when at least one active space opens on it, and the booking form lists only the spaces open that day. A day on which no space opens renders closed and cannot be picked.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Admin bypass and Override Conflicts', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Each calendar has an Admin bypass toggle (General tab, on by default). While it is on, holders of manage_options or ffc_bypass_audiences may open closed days and book any space, the closed ones marked with their reasons (closed, holiday, outside hours, past date, beyond the booking window). Turn it off to apply every rule to them too.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A user whose User access row carries Override Conflicts, or a bypass holder, may book over an occupied slot, after writing why. The earlier booking loses only the days that clash: its other days stay booked, with their attendance and post-event invitations, and a booking left with no day is cancelled. Its owner receives the "booking cancelled by an administrator" email naming the days and the reason, and its participants the usual cancellation notice. The hours of each day of a booking that spans several days are stored when it is made, so editing a space\'s working hours later does not move bookings already made.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Either way the booking first opens one warning listing every rule it breaks, conflicts included. Its "I agree" button unlocks after 15 seconds and Cancel works at once; the server saves nothing without that confirmation, and each confirmed booking is written to the Activity Log with its reasons.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Notifications', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Each schedule independently notifies affected users on booking and cancellation, and can attach an ICS calendar invite. Two opt-in admin toggles (off by default) also notify a recipient list (comma-separated; blank falls back to the site admin) on new bookings and cancellations.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'These e-mails leave in the background: saving or cancelling a booking only queues them, and WP-Cron sends them in batches of 50, each person once. With Total Mail Queue active they join its queue, attributed to Audience Calendars. On a site whose WP-Cron runs only on visits, set the server cron line from Settings → Scheduled Tasks so they do not wait for traffic.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A booking is cancelled once. A second request for the same booking, from another tab or another person, answers that it is already cancelled and sends no e-mail. A booking and its participants are saved together: if one of them cannot be written, nothing is saved and the person sees an error.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Admin pages', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The Scheduling menu lists Dashboard, Personal Calendars, Appointments, Audience Calendars, Audiences, Audience Bookings and Settings. A new personal calendar is added with the Add New button of Personal Calendars or the Dashboard\'s quick action.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Audience Calendars has two views, switched by the links under its heading: Calendars and Environments (named with the label you give environments). The environment count in a calendar\'s row opens that calendar\'s environments.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'Add New on a calendar\'s Environments tab, or on the Environments view filtered to one calendar, opens the new environment with that calendar already chosen.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'An environment is deactivated before it can be deleted, and one that any booking names, past or cancelled included, cannot be deleted at all: it stays inactive, so it takes no new bookings and its bookings keep their place.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'The same holds one level up. A calendar that still has environments or bookings cannot be deleted; the message names how many, and an environment without bookings can be deleted first. An audience that a booking names, directly or through one of its groups, cannot be deleted either, because deleting it would also remove its groups and memberships and shrink the participant list of those bookings after the fact. Deactivate it instead; the list shows "Has bookings, kept" in place of Delete.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Audience Bookings lists the bookings 50 to a page, newest first, with their calendar, who each is for and how many people checked in out of how many it reaches. Filter by calendar, space, audience, status and dates, or search the description or the booking number; the CSV export (ffc_export_audiences) takes the same filters. Holders of ffc_manage_audiences can tick active bookings and cancel them together with one reason; each cancelled booking e-mails its participants as a single cancellation does. Global holidays, set on the Scheduling → Settings → General tab, block bookings on those dates.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A closed date, global or on a calendar\'s Closed dates tab, is a holiday or an optional holiday. Both block bookings the same way; the public calendars label them apart, in the day cell, the legend and the event list. Calendar Colors and Labels, on the same General tab, sets the light-theme color and the names of each state both calendars draw: available, booked, holiday, optional holiday and closed. An empty name keeps the default, the text on each color turns white or black to stay readable, and a switch hides the closed day\'s name from its cells.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A date that is already closed, on the same calendar or in the global list, is refused with a message. Closing a date that already holds audience bookings asks first: the screen lists those bookings, and "Close the day and cancel the bookings" saves the date and cancels each one with the reason "Closed day:" followed by the description, e-mailing its participants as any cancellation does. A calendar\'s closed date takes that calendar\'s bookings; a global holiday takes the bookings of every audience calendar. Personal-calendar appointments on that date are not cancelled.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The Booked row also names the bookings badge, in the singular and the plural ("1 booking", "2 bookings"). An audience calendar\'s own booking label, on its settings tab, wins over these names. The Available row has no day cell name by default; one set there shows on available days that have no bookings yet.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Those are the general names. A calendar that needs its own sets them on the calendar: the Names tab of a personal calendar, and the Calendar names field on an audience calendar\'s settings tab. Each field there shows the general name it replaces; an empty field keeps it. The calendar\'s name wins over the general one, which wins over the default, and the same goes for showing or hiding the closed day\'s name (General, Show or Hide). Colors stay general. A page that shows several audience calendars takes, per name, the first calendar that sets one.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Each closed date in the two lists has an Edit button, which opens the row in place to change its date, type or description. A date another closed date of the same list already uses is refused.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Attendance and QR check-in', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The Attendance link in a booking\'s row of Audience Bookings opens its attendance screen. On the day itself, until 30 minutes after the booking ends, Open check-in starts a check-in for that day: choose how often the code changes (1, 2, 3, 5 or 10 minutes) and, optionally, the saved geolocation areas the participant must be inside. Project the QR code opens a full-screen page for a projector or a screen; the code changes on its own and the page counts who is present. Check-in closes when you press Close check-in, or on its own 30 minutes after the booking ends that day. Opening it again starts a new check-in, and codes from the previous one stop working.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A participant scans the code with the phone camera, logs in if asked and confirms. Only people on the booking (its custom users and the members of its audiences) can check in, once per day. A code shown on the screen is valid for its rotation and the one before; a link copied from it expires within minutes. The location of an area check-in comes from the participant\'s browser and can be faked: it makes checking in from elsewhere harder, it does not prove presence.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A scan is exchanged for a link that stays valid for two rotations of the code (2 minutes when the code changes every minute, 20 minutes when it changes every 10), long enough to log in. Without a geolocation area a check-in proves that the person saw the code, not that they were in the room: a photo of the screen sent to someone elsewhere works while the code is live. For a physical room, choose the room\'s area when opening the check-in.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'When the check-in asks for a location, the page runs the same checks a certificate form runs before it is used: a page not served over HTTPS is stopped, because the browser gives no location there, and an app\'s own browser (Instagram, WhatsApp and others) is asked to open the page in the phone\'s browser, with "Continue anyway". A location already refused in the browser is said at once, before the tap. A reading is judged against each area as on a certificate form, with the same accepted error; one that cannot tell inside from outside is refused; there is no "When GPS fails" policy here: without a usable location there is no check-in, and the organiser can mark attendance by hand. Each stop is written to the Activity Log as a pre-flight block naming the booking.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The participants list shows who is present on the day selected and refreshes on its own. Mark present and Remove record or delete an attendance by hand on today or an earlier day, noting who marked it. The attendance rule decides who counts as attended for a booking over several days: Any day, or a minimum share of the booked days (skipped days do not count). Export CSV lists each participant\'s name, a column per day, the days present and whether the rule was met, and, when a post-event form applies, when they were invited and whether they answered; it carries no e-mail or document number.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Attendance lines are records: when an account is deleted or its data erased, the line stays and loses the person. A participant\'s attendance is included in their privacy export.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Post-event certificate form', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The Post-event form tab of a calendar names the certificate form its bookings lead to, how many days it stays open after the event (1 to 90) and whether only people whose attendance meets the booking\'s rule are invited. The form opens when the booking\'s last day ends. The questions are ordinary fields of that certificate form.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The Post-event form card on a booking\'s Attendance screen says which form applies and when it is open. Its switch gives that booking settings of its own instead of its calendar\'s, including None to invite nobody; changing it needs ffc_manage_audiences. This card and the Attendance rule card save each change as it is made, and the sentences describing them follow; reload the page to see the invitations.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'An invitation link opens the form with the participant\'s name and e-mail filled in and locked, from their account; no login is needed, so treat the link as personal. The CPF/RF is not filled in: the participant types it, and it is refused when it belongs to another account, or when it differs from the one already on record. A first CPF/RF is accepted and recorded on their profile. The submission keeps the booking it came from. To keep the form closed to everyone else, switch on its "Booking participants only" restriction.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Once an hour the plugin e-mails each eligible participant of a booking whose form has opened the personal link, once. The e-mail is "Audience post-event form invitation" under Settings → Email texts. It needs a published page that embeds the form, since the link points there; without one nothing is sent. The Attendance screen lists who was invited and when, who answered and whose certificate was issued, and while the form is open it resends to one person or to everyone who has not answered; this needs ffc_checkin_audiences.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'A participant whose e-mail went astray also finds the form on their dashboard, in the bookings tab, until it closes or they answer.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Import / export', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'The Scheduling → Settings → Import & Export tab imports and exports audiences (name, color, parent) and members (email, name, audience) as CSV, optionally creating WordPress users for imported members.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>
	<p><?php esc_html_e( 'Importing needs ffc_import_audiences, which the Audience Calendars Operator role holds. An Operator can therefore add members to any audience and, with the option on, create WordPress accounts for addresses that have none; give the role only to people trusted with that.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></p>

	<h4><?php esc_html_e( 'Capabilities', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></h4>
	<ul>
		<li><code>ffc_view_audiences</code> — <?php esc_html_e( 'view the Scheduling menu and audience pages.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?> <?php esc_html_e( 'On its own it opens them read-only: the calendar, environment and audience edit forms and the members screen show their fields disabled, with no Save, Add or Remove, under a read-only notice.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></li>
		<li><code>ffc_manage_audiences</code> — <?php esc_html_e( 'create audiences, schedules, environments and members.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></li>
		<li><code>ffc_import_audiences</code> / <code>ffc_export_audiences</code> / <code>ffc_delete_audiences</code> — <?php esc_html_e( 'import, export, delete.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></li>
		<li><code>ffc_checkin_audiences</code> — <?php esc_html_e( 'run attendance: open and close check-in, project the QR code and mark attendance by hand. Held by the Operator tier and above. Changing a booking\'s attendance rule needs ffc_manage_audiences, and its CSV needs ffc_export_audiences.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></li>
		<li><code>ffc_view_audiences_pii</code> — <?php esc_html_e( 'reveal the account email of the people on the Members and User access tabs and in a booking\'s details, and the sensitive custom fields on another user\'s profile, with an audited Reveal; a members export carries the email in clear only with it. Existing holders of ffc_manage_audiences received it on update.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></li>
		<li><code>ffc_bypass_audiences</code> — <?php esc_html_e( 'book outside the calendar rules and over an occupied slot, after the warning, and cancel anyone\'s booking, on calendars whose Admin bypass toggle is on; also see every calendar. With the toggle off, cancelling someone else\'s booking needs Cancel Others on the calendar, as for anyone. Held by the FFC Audience Calendars - Administrator role and FFC Administrator. ffc_bypass_appointments no longer reaches audience calendars.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></li>
		<li><code>ffc_view_own_audience_bookings</code> — <?php esc_html_e( 'the end-user cap; booking/cancel rights come from per-schedule permissions.', 'ffcertificate' ); ?></li>
	</ul>
</div>
